1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
A gardener takes cuttings
from one hydrangea, so

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,840
every new plant is a clone, with exactly

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:07,080
the same genes.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,560
Planted in two different
gardens, one flowers blue,

5
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:12,200
and one flowers pink.

6
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,000
Last time, a ribosome built a chain of

7
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,320
amino acids.

8
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:18,480
So how does a chain like that become

9
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,560
a trait you can see, and how can

10
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,880
the same genes give different results?

11
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,180
Most of a cell's jobs are done by

12
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:27,460
proteins.

13
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:30,020
Enzymes speed up reactions.

14
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,340
Transport proteins carry things,
like haemoglobin carrying oxygen

15
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:35,940
in red blood cells.

16
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:39,060
Structural proteins give
strength, like the keratin in

17
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,260
your hair.

18
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:43,300
Receptors on a cell's
surface pick up signals,

19
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:46,180
and antibodies grab onto germs.

20
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:48,340
Every one of these is built from one

21
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:51,780
or more polypeptide
chains, folded into its own

22
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:52,340
shape.

23
00:00:53,567 --> 00:00:55,806
So why does a chain fold into one

24
00:00:55,887 --> 00:00:57,086
particular shape?

25
00:00:57,327 --> 00:01:00,126
Because the twenty amino
acids are different.

26
00:01:00,327 --> 00:01:02,686
Some avoid water, so they tuck into the

27
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:04,846
middle of the chain, away from the watery

28
00:01:04,927 --> 00:01:05,726
cell.

29
00:01:05,887 --> 00:01:09,326
Some carry a charge, and
opposite charges attract.

30
00:01:09,447 --> 00:01:12,046
And two amino acids
called cysteine can lock

31
00:01:12,127 --> 00:01:14,846
together with a strong
link called a disulfide

32
00:01:14,927 --> 00:01:15,726
bond.

33
00:01:15,807 --> 00:01:18,366
The order of amino acids
decides which parts

34
00:01:18,447 --> 00:01:20,446
end up next to each other, so it

35
00:01:20,527 --> 00:01:21,646
decides the shape.

36
00:01:22,867 --> 00:01:26,426
Shape matters because
proteins work by fitting.

37
00:01:26,707 --> 00:01:29,666
An enzyme's substrate
fits into a pocket, its

38
00:01:29,827 --> 00:01:32,226
active site, like a key in a lock,

39
00:01:32,307 --> 00:01:33,986
though the protein can flex a little as

40
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:34,786
it binds.

41
00:01:35,427 --> 00:01:37,906
Change one amino acid in that pocket, and

42
00:01:37,947 --> 00:01:40,306
the shape can change so the substrate no

43
00:01:40,387 --> 00:01:41,426
longer fits.

44
00:01:41,747 --> 00:01:43,586
But not every change matters.

45
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:46,066
A swap far from the pocket, or to

46
00:01:46,147 --> 00:01:48,466
a similar amino acid, may leave the shape

47
00:01:48,547 --> 00:01:49,106
working.

48
00:01:49,507 --> 00:01:51,986
And many base changes
don't change the amino

49
00:01:52,107 --> 00:01:52,946
acid at all.

50
00:01:54,167 --> 00:01:56,726
Let's follow one real case all the way.

51
00:01:57,287 --> 00:02:00,006
Haemoglobin is built from four chains.

52
00:02:00,247 --> 00:02:01,766
In the gene for one of them, the

53
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:04,646
beta chain, a single base change turns the

54
00:02:04,727 --> 00:02:07,926
codon G A G into G U G

55
00:02:08,087 --> 00:02:09,446
in the mRNA.

56
00:02:09,767 --> 00:02:12,486
The chart gives valine
instead of glutamate, at

57
00:02:12,527 --> 00:02:13,766
the sixth amino acid.

58
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:17,446
So one base changes one amino acid, out

59
00:02:17,527 --> 00:02:19,926
of a chain of a hundred and forty-six.

60
00:02:21,134 --> 00:02:25,173
Glutamate carries a charge,
but valine avoids water.

61
00:02:25,454 --> 00:02:27,293
So in a person with two copies of

62
00:02:27,374 --> 00:02:30,413
this allele, when oxygen
is low, the changed

63
00:02:30,494 --> 00:02:33,373
haemoglobin molecules stick
to each other and stack

64
00:02:33,534 --> 00:02:35,693
into long stiff fibres.

65
00:02:35,774 --> 00:02:37,773
The fibres bend the red blood cells into

66
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:39,293
a sickle shape.

67
00:02:39,334 --> 00:02:41,853
Sickle cells are stiff,
so they block small

68
00:02:41,974 --> 00:02:44,813
blood vessels and cause
pain, and they break

69
00:02:44,974 --> 00:02:47,053
down quickly, so there are too few red

70
00:02:47,494 --> 00:02:48,013
cells.

71
00:02:48,094 --> 00:02:50,813
That whole pattern is
the trait, sickle cell

72
00:02:50,854 --> 00:02:51,533
disease.

73
00:02:52,734 --> 00:02:54,653
Notice how many steps that took.

74
00:02:55,214 --> 00:02:57,293
Genes don't become traits directly.

75
00:02:57,774 --> 00:03:00,493
A gene codes for a
polypeptide, which folds

76
00:03:00,574 --> 00:03:03,053
into a protein, which changes what a cell

77
00:03:03,134 --> 00:03:06,173
does, and that shows up as a trait.

78
00:03:06,294 --> 00:03:08,893
Your set of alleles is your genotype.

79
00:03:08,974 --> 00:03:11,053
The traits you can observe, from a cell's

80
00:03:11,254 --> 00:03:13,133
shape to the colour of a flower, are

81
00:03:13,214 --> 00:03:14,573
your phenotype.

82
00:03:14,694 --> 00:03:17,693
And most traits involve
many proteins, so many

83
00:03:17,854 --> 00:03:19,213
genes, at once.

84
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:22,313
Now back to the hydrangeas.

85
00:03:22,594 --> 00:03:24,833
Their genes code for the enzymes that make

86
00:03:24,914 --> 00:03:26,673
a red pigment in the flowers.

87
00:03:27,234 --> 00:03:30,433
In acidic soil, aluminium
dissolves, and the roots

88
00:03:30,554 --> 00:03:31,073
take it up.

89
00:03:31,634 --> 00:03:34,433
In the flowers, aluminium
binds to the pigment

90
00:03:34,594 --> 00:03:35,633
and turns it blue.

91
00:03:36,354 --> 00:03:39,793
In less acidic soil, little
aluminium dissolves, so

92
00:03:39,874 --> 00:03:41,553
the same pigment stays pink.

93
00:03:42,194 --> 00:03:43,873
The genotype is the same.

94
00:03:44,194 --> 00:03:46,593
The environment changes what it produces.

95
00:03:47,314 --> 00:03:48,833
Height works the same way.

96
00:03:49,234 --> 00:03:51,873
It depends on many genes, but children who

97
00:03:51,954 --> 00:03:54,113
don't get enough food
don't reach the height

98
00:03:54,194 --> 00:03:55,393
their genes allow.

99
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:59,000
So how much is genes, and how much

100
00:03:59,040 --> 00:03:59,880
is environment?

101
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:03,640
Genes set what's possible,
and the environment affects

102
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:06,280
what actually develops within that range.

103
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:08,760
The balance differs between traits.

104
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,080
Your ABO blood group is set by your

105
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:14,040
alleles, and food or
climate won't change it.

106
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:16,280
Height depends on both.

107
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:19,080
And the environment
changes the trait, not the

108
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:20,360
genes themselves.

109
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,440
Take a cutting from the blue plant and

110
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,680
grow it in less acidic soil, and its

111
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:26,280
new flowers come out pink.

112
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:30,780
That's why making
polypeptides matters so much.

113
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:33,580
Almost everything a
cell does depends on its

114
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:37,260
proteins, and every protein
is made by transcription

115
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:38,860
and translation.

116
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,820
Your cells all carry the same genes, but

117
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:43,900
each kind of cell makes its own set

118
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:46,220
of proteins, which is why a red blood

119
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:48,860
cell fills with haemoglobin
as it develops, and

120
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,780
a muscle cell doesn't.

121
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:52,940
If the sequence is wrong, or the protein

122
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:55,580
isn't made, the job doesn't get done.

123
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:57,680
Two aids.

124
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:01,680
For protein structure:
one, the order of amino

125
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:06,800
acids; two, local coils and folds; three,

126
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:10,400
the whole folded shape of one chain; four,

127
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:13,360
more than one chain
together, like haemoglobin.

128
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:17,120
And for traits: genes
set what's possible, and

129
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:20,000
the environment affects
what actually develops.

130
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,880
Two harder questions now.

131
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:26,560
First, Himalayan rabbits
have white fur on the

132
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:29,840
body, but dark fur on the ears, nose,

133
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:30,960
feet and tail.

134
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:34,000
The dark pigment is made by an enzyme.

135
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,360
Scientists shaved a patch
on one rabbit's back

136
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:39,120
and kept an ice pack on it while

137
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:40,080
the fur grew back.

138
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:42,320
The new fur grew dark.

139
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,120
Explain why the new fur is dark.

140
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:48,400
Then predict the back fur of its young,

141
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:50,720
which also have the
Himalayan genotype and are

142
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,520
raised normally, and
justify your prediction.

143
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:55,440
Pause here to answer.

144
00:06:05,867 --> 00:06:07,946
The dark parts are the coolest parts of

145
00:06:07,987 --> 00:06:10,426
the body, so the enzyme must work only

146
00:06:10,507 --> 00:06:12,186
where the skin is cool.

147
00:06:12,267 --> 00:06:14,426
On the warm back it doesn't work, so

148
00:06:14,507 --> 00:06:16,106
no pigment forms.

149
00:06:16,147 --> 00:06:18,426
The ice pack cooled the skin, the enzyme

150
00:06:18,507 --> 00:06:20,786
worked, and the fur grew dark.

151
00:06:21,067 --> 00:06:23,146
The young will have
white backs, because the

152
00:06:23,227 --> 00:06:25,986
cold changed where the
enzyme worked, not the

153
00:06:26,027 --> 00:06:27,266
gene they inherit.

154
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:29,546
If you said the ice changed the rabbit's

155
00:06:29,787 --> 00:06:31,226
genes, look again.

156
00:06:32,434 --> 00:06:32,993
Second.

157
00:06:33,474 --> 00:06:36,753
In snapdragons, red
petals need an enzyme that

158
00:06:36,834 --> 00:06:38,193
helps make red pigment.

159
00:06:38,754 --> 00:06:41,153
Two plants each carry two copies of a

160
00:06:41,314 --> 00:06:43,473
different change in this enzyme's gene.

161
00:06:43,954 --> 00:06:47,313
In plant P, the mRNA codon for an

162
00:06:47,394 --> 00:06:50,033
amino acid in the active site changes from

163
00:06:50,194 --> 00:06:52,873
C A U to C C U.

164
00:06:53,154 --> 00:06:55,633
In plant Q, a codon far from the

165
00:06:55,714 --> 00:06:58,673
active site changes from G A A to

166
00:06:58,834 --> 00:07:00,193
G A G.

167
00:07:00,514 --> 00:07:03,473
Plant P has white flowers, and plant Q

168
00:07:03,554 --> 00:07:04,673
has red flowers.

169
00:07:05,234 --> 00:07:07,073
Use the chart to explain both.

170
00:07:07,554 --> 00:07:08,833
Pause here to answer.

171
00:07:19,234 --> 00:07:22,353
In plant P, C A U is histidine

172
00:07:22,594 --> 00:07:25,153
and C C U is proline, so a

173
00:07:25,314 --> 00:07:27,873
different amino acid
sits in the active site.

174
00:07:28,114 --> 00:07:30,913
The pocket's shape changes,
the enzyme can't hold

175
00:07:30,994 --> 00:07:33,953
its substrate, and no
red pigment forms, so

176
00:07:33,994 --> 00:07:35,193
the flowers are white.

177
00:07:35,474 --> 00:07:38,273
In plant Q, G A A and G

178
00:07:38,354 --> 00:07:41,153
A G both code for glutamate, so the

179
00:07:41,234 --> 00:07:44,113
enzyme is unchanged and
the flowers stay red.

180
00:07:44,434 --> 00:07:46,513
If you said every change to a gene

181
00:07:46,594 --> 00:07:49,473
changes the protein,
check the chart first.

182
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,380
So when a question links a gene to

183
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:55,740
a trait, walk the chain: the base, the

184
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:59,060
codon, the amino acid, the shape, what the

185
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:01,300
cell does, then the trait.

186
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:04,220
And when the same genes
give different results,

187
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,620
look for what the environment is changing.

188
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:09,820
Next, where the different
genotypes in one family

189
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:11,420
come from in the first place.

190
00:08:12,634 --> 00:08:14,073
Here are the key notes.

191
00:08:14,314 --> 00:08:16,233
Pause the video if you need more time

192
00:08:16,314 --> 00:08:17,113
to copy them.
